('node-13-16', 'phase-13-lesson-16-track-d-alignment-science-direct-preferen', 'module-14', 16, 'Lesson 14.16: Track D: Alignment Science: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) & RLHF', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Phase 10', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Phase 10 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 360, 14, 3310.0, 800.0, '# Lesson 13.16: Track D: Alignment Science: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) & RLHF

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 9, Phase 10
- **Subtopics**:
  - `13.16.1` The alignment problem: steering raw pre-trained next-token predictors toward helpful, harmless, and honest behavior
  - `13.16.2` Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) classical pipeline: SFT $\to$ Reward Model Training $\to$ PPO Policy Optimization
  - `13.16.3` Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) complexity: coordinating 4 models concurrently (Policy, Value, Reference, Reward), training instability
  - `13.16.4` Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) breakthrough (Rafailov et al.): mathematically deriving closed-form policy loss without a reward model
  - `13.16.5` DPO loss formulation: $\mathcal{L}_{DPO}(\pi_\theta; \pi_{ref}) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\log \sigma\left(\beta \log \frac{\pi_\theta(y_w|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_w|x)} - \beta \log \frac{\pi_\theta(y_l|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_l|x)}\right)\right]$
  - `13.16.6` KTO (Kahneman-Tversky Optimization) and ORPO (Odds Ratio Preference Optimization): preference optimization without paired datasets
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Setting DPO temperature parameter $\beta$ too high or low, causing model mode collapse or failing to learn user preferences.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement the DPO loss function from scratch in PyTorch; fine-tune an aligned model on paired preference data.
- **Project Application**: Track D Capstone: DPO model alignment and preference optimization pipeline.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 13 // Lesson 13.16: Track D: Alignment Science: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) & RLHF\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement the DPO loss function from scratch in PyTorch; fine-tune an aligned model on paired prefer\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "13.16", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Implement the DPO loss function from scratch in PyTorch; fine-tune an aligned model on paired preference data.", "subtopics": ["13.16.1 The alignment problem: steering raw pre-trained next-token predictors toward helpful, harmless, and honest behavior", "13.16.2 Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) classical pipeline: SFT $\\to$ Reward Model Training $\\to$ PPO Policy Optimization", "13.16.3 Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) complexity: coordinating 4 models concurrently (Policy, Value, Reference, Reward), training instability", "13.16.4 Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) breakthrough (Rafailov et al.): mathematically deriving closed-form policy loss without a reward model", "13.16.5 DPO loss formulation: $\\mathcal{L}_{DPO}(\\pi_\\theta; \\pi_{ref}) = -\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\log \\sigma\\left(\\beta \\log \\frac{\\pi_\\theta(y_w|x)}{\\pi_{ref}(y_w|x)} - \\beta \\log \\frac{\\pi_\\theta(y_l|x)}{\\pi_{ref}(y_l|x)}\\right)\\right]$", "13.16.6 KTO (Kahneman-Tversky Optimization) and ORPO (Odds Ratio Preference Optimization): preference optimization without paired datasets"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Setting DPO temperature parameter $\\beta$ too high or low, causing model mode collapse or failing to learn user preferen", "How does Track D: Alignment Science: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) & RLHF scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-13-17', 'phase-13-lesson-17-track-d-mixture-of-experts-moe-architectu', 'module-14', 17, 'Lesson 14.17: Track D: Mixture of Experts (MoE) Architecture: Routing & Sparse Compute', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Lesson 13.7', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Lesson 13.7 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 360, 14, 3370.0, 850.0, '# Lesson 13.17: Track D: Mixture of Experts (MoE) Architecture: Routing & Sparse Compute

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 9, Lesson 13.7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `13.17.1` Dense models vs Sparse Mixture of Experts (MoE): scaling parameter capacity exponentially while keeping inference compute constant
  - `13.17.2` MoE layer architecture: replacing standard FFN layers with $N$ parallel expert FFNs and a learnable gating router network
  - `13.17.3` Top-$k$ routing mechanics (Shazeer et al.): router projecting token embedding to logits, selecting top $k$ experts via softmax (typically $k=2$)
  - `13.17.4` Expert capacity and load balancing: auxiliary load balancing loss (Switch Transformer) preventing routing collapse to single popular expert
  - `13.17.5` Token dropping vs padding: managing fixed expert buffer capacities during batched execution across distributed GPU nodes
  - `13.17.6` DeepSeek-V2/V3 and Mixtral architecture: fine-grained experts, shared experts, and multi-token prediction heads
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Router collapsing to 1 or 2 dominant experts due to missing load balancing loss, leaving remaining experts untrained.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Build a Top-2 Mixture of Experts (MoE) transformer layer with auxiliary load balancing loss from scratch in PyTorch.
- **Project Application**: Track D Capstone: Sparse Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 13 // Lesson 13.17: Track D: Mixture of Experts (MoE) Architecture: Routing & Sparse Compute\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Build a Top-2 Mixture of Experts (MoE) transformer layer with auxiliary load balancing loss from scr\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "13.17", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Build a Top-2 Mixture of Experts (MoE) transformer layer with auxiliary load balancing loss from scratch in PyTorch.", "subtopics": ["13.17.1 Dense models vs Sparse Mixture of Experts (MoE): scaling parameter capacity exponentially while keeping inference compute constant", "13.17.2 MoE layer architecture: replacing standard FFN layers with $N$ parallel expert FFNs and a learnable gating router network", "13.17.3 Top-$k$ routing mechanics (Shazeer et al.): router projecting token embedding to logits, selecting top $k$ experts via softmax (typically $k=2$)", "13.17.4 Expert capacity and load balancing: auxiliary load balancing loss (Switch Transformer) preventing routing collapse to single popular expert", "13.17.5 Token dropping vs padding: managing fixed expert buffer capacities during batched execution across distributed GPU nodes", "13.17.6 DeepSeek-V2/V3 and Mixtral architecture: fine-grained experts, shared experts, and multi-token prediction heads"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Router collapsing to 1 or 2 dominant experts due to missing load balancing loss, leaving remaining experts untrained.", "How does Track D: Mixture of Experts (MoE) Architecture: Routing & Sparse Compute scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-13-18', 'phase-13-lesson-18-track-d-state-space-models-ssms-modern-ma', 'module-14', 18, 'Lesson 14.18: Track D: State Space Models (SSMs) & Modern Mamba Architecture', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Phase 10', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Phase 10 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 360, 14, 3250.0, 900.0, '# Lesson 13.18: Track D: State Space Models (SSMs) & Modern Mamba Architecture

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 9, Phase 10
- **Subtopics**:
  - `13.18.1` The quadratic bottleneck of Transformers: $O(T^2)$ computational complexity of self-attention limiting long-context scalability
  - `13.18.2` Classical State Space Models: continuous differential equations mapping 1D stimulus $x(t) \to y(t)$ via latent state $h(t)$
  - `13.18.3` HiPPO framework (High-order Polynomial Projection Operators): memory initialization maintaining continuous signal history
  - `13.18.4` Structured State Space Models (S4): continuous to discrete discretization (Bilinear / Euler transform) and fast convolutional training
  - `13.18.5` Mamba architecture (Gu & Dao): Selective State Spaces (S6) making transition matrices input-dependent to filter irrelevant information
  - `13.18.6` Hardware-aware selective scan: fused GPU SRAM kernel eliminating slow HBM transfers, achieving linear $O(T)$ training and $O(1)$ inference
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Attempting to train S4/Mamba with standard non-fused PyTorch scan operators, causing massive memory overhead and GPU stalling.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement a discrete State Space Model forward pass in PyTorch; verify linear $O(T)$ memory scaling on 10,000 token sequences.
- **Project Application**: Track D Capstone: Selective State Space (Mamba) sequence model.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 13 // Lesson 13.18: Track D: State Space Models (SSMs) & Modern Mamba Architecture\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement a discrete State Space Model forward pass in PyTorch; verify linear $O(T)$ memory scaling \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "13.18", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Implement a discrete State Space Model forward pass in PyTorch; verify linear $O(T)$ memory scaling on 10,000 token sequences.", "subtopics": ["13.18.1 The quadratic bottleneck of Transformers: $O(T^2)$ computational complexity of self-attention limiting long-context scalability", "13.18.2 Classical State Space Models: continuous differential equations mapping 1D stimulus $x(t) \\to y(t)$ via latent state $h(t)$", "13.18.3 HiPPO framework (High-order Polynomial Projection Operators): memory initialization maintaining continuous signal history", "13.18.4 Structured State Space Models (S4): continuous to discrete discretization (Bilinear / Euler transform) and fast convolutional training", "13.18.5 Mamba architecture (Gu & Dao): Selective State Spaces (S6) making transition matrices input-dependent to filter irrelevant information", "13.18.6 Hardware-aware selective scan: fused GPU SRAM kernel eliminating slow HBM transfers, achieving linear $O(T)$ training and $O(1)$ inference"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Attempting to train S4/Mamba with standard non-fused PyTorch scan operators, causing massive memory overhead and GPU sta", "How does Track D: State Space Models (SSMs) & Modern Mamba Architecture scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-13-19', 'phase-13-lesson-19-track-d-mechanistic-interpretability-prob', 'module-14', 19, 'Lesson 14.19: Track D: Mechanistic Interpretability: Probing Features & Induction Heads', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Phase 10', 'Prerequisites: Phase 9, Phase 10 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 360, 14, 3310.0, 950.0, '# Lesson 13.19: Track D: Mechanistic Interpretability: Probing Features & Induction Heads

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 9, Phase 10
- **Subtopics**:
  - `13.19.1` The mechanistic interpretability goal: reverse-engineering the internal weights and circuits of neural networks into human-understandable algorithms
  - `13.19.2` Linear Artificial Neurons vs Polysemanticity: single neurons firing for completely unrelated concepts due to superposition
  - `13.19.3` Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) (Anthropic): decomposing internal dense activations into sparse, monosemantic feature dictionaries
  - `13.19.4` Attention circuit analysis (Elhage et al.): QK-circuits (where tokens attend) vs OV-circuits (what information is moved)
  - `13.19.5` Induction Heads: two-layer attention circuits that implement in-context pattern completion ($[A][B] \dots [A] \to [B]$)
  - `13.19.6` Activation patching and causal scrubbing: isolating exact neural sub-circuits responsible for specific factual recall and reasoning
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Assuming individual neurons map to single human concepts, ignoring superposition and cross-layer feature interference.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Train a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) on transformer hidden states; extract and visualize monosemantic features and verify induction heads.
- **Project Application**: Track D Capstone: Mechanistic interpretability and SAE feature extraction harness.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 13 // Lesson 13.19: Track D: Mechanistic Interpretability: Probing Features & Induction Heads\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Train a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) on transformer hidden states; extract and visualize monosemantic fe\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "13.19", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Train a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) on transformer hidden states; extract and visualize monosemantic features and verify induction heads.", "subtopics": ["13.19.1 The mechanistic interpretability goal: reverse-engineering the internal weights and circuits of neural networks into human-understandable algorithms", "13.19.2 Linear Artificial Neurons vs Polysemanticity: single neurons firing for completely unrelated concepts due to superposition", "13.19.3 Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) (Anthropic): decomposing internal dense activations into sparse, monosemantic feature dictionaries", "13.19.4 Attention circuit analysis (Elhage et al.): QK-circuits (where tokens attend) vs OV-circuits (what information is moved)", "13.19.5 Induction Heads: two-layer attention circuits that implement in-context pattern completion ($[A][B] \\dots [A] \\to [B]$)", "13.19.6 Activation patching and causal scrubbing: isolating exact neural sub-circuits responsible for specific factual recall and reasoning"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Assuming individual neurons map to single human concepts, ignoring superposition and cross-layer feature interference.", "How does Track D: Mechanistic Interpretability: Probing Features & Induction Heads scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-13-20', 'phase-13-lesson-20-track-d-frontier-model-quantization-custo', 'module-14', 20, 'Lesson 14.20: Track D: Frontier Model Quantization & Custom Kernel Engineering (Triton)', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Phase 9, Phase 11', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Phase 9, Phase 11 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 360, 14, 3370.0, 1000.0, '# Lesson 13.20: Track D: Frontier Model Quantization & Custom Kernel Engineering (Triton)

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0, Phase 9, Phase 11
- **Subtopics**:
  - `13.20.1` The memory wall in deep learning: compute speed exceeding GPU memory bandwidth; arithmetic intensity optimization
  - `13.20.2` OpenAI Triton language: Python-based programming model for writing high-performance GPU kernels without writing raw CUDA C++
  - `13.20.3` Triton programming mental model: block-level programming, automated shared memory management, and coalesced global memory loads
  - `13.20.4` Writing a custom fused Softmax kernel in Triton: loading blocks into SRAM, vectorized operations, and storing results with minimal HBM passes
  - `13.20.5` Writing a fused FlashAttention kernel in Triton: tiling Query, Key, Value blocks, online softmax scaling, and zero materialized $T \times T$ matrix
  - `13.20.6` Benchmarking custom Triton kernels: achieving $> 80\%$ of theoretical peak hardware TFLOPs on modern NVIDIA GPUs
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Failing to handle non-power-of-two tensor boundaries in Triton block masking, producing silent memory corruption.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Write and benchmark a custom fused FlashAttention kernel in OpenAI Triton; demonstrate $3\times$ speedup over native PyTorch attention.
- **Project Application**: Track D Capstone: Custom fused FlashAttention Triton kernel.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 13 // Lesson 13.20: Track D: Frontier Model Quantization & Custom Kernel Engineering (Triton)\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Write and benchmark a custom fused FlashAttention kernel in OpenAI Triton; demonstrate $3\\times$ spe\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "13.20", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Write and benchmark a custom fused FlashAttention kernel in OpenAI Triton; demonstrate $3\\times$ speedup over native PyTorch attention.", "subtopics": ["13.20.1 The memory wall in deep learning: compute speed exceeding GPU memory bandwidth; arithmetic intensity optimization", "13.20.2 OpenAI Triton language: Python-based programming model for writing high-performance GPU kernels without writing raw CUDA C++", "13.20.3 Triton programming mental model: block-level programming, automated shared memory management, and coalesced global memory loads", "13.20.4 Writing a custom fused Softmax kernel in Triton: loading blocks into SRAM, vectorized operations, and storing results with minimal HBM passes", "13.20.5 Writing a fused FlashAttention kernel in Triton: tiling Query, Key, Value blocks, online softmax scaling, and zero materialized $T \\times T$ matrix", "13.20.6 Benchmarking custom Triton kernels: achieving $> 80\\%$ of theoretical peak hardware TFLOPs on modern NVIDIA GPUs"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Failing to handle non-power-of-two tensor boundaries in Triton block masking, producing silent memory corruption.", "How does Track D: Frontier Model Quantization & Custom Kernel Engineering (Triton) scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-14-1', 'phase-14-lesson-01-enterprise-capstone-i-system-architecture', 'module-14', 21, 'Lesson 14.21: Enterprise Capstone I: System Architecture & Non-Functional Requirements', 'Prerequisites: Phase 8, Phase 12', 'Prerequisites: Phase 8, Phase 12 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 380, 15, 3560.0, 50.0, '# Lesson 14.1: Enterprise Capstone I: System Architecture & Non-Functional Requirements

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 8, Phase 12
- **Subtopics**:
  - `14.1.1` Defining the enterprise project charter: building a multi-tenant, autonomous software intelligence and operations platform
  - `14.1.2` Scoping functional requirements: automated code review, security remediation, real-time telemetry analytics, autonomous bug repair
  - `14.1.3` Scoping non-functional requirements: $99.99\%$ uptime SLA, sub-200ms API p99 latency, zero cross-tenant data leakage
  - `14.1.4` Architecture Decision Records (ADRs): documenting technology choices across database, frontend, messaging, and AI models
  - `14.1.5` Threat modeling: defining security boundaries, zero-trust network policies, and PII compliance controls
  - `14.1.6` Creating the multi-repository vs monorepo workspace structure with Turborepo and strict dependency boundaries
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Vague architectural boundaries leading to monolithic spaghetti code and circular dependencies between services.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Author the complete System Architecture Specification and ADR documentation; pass an initial architectural review.
- **Project Application**: Enterprise Capstone: System architecture document and ADRs.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 14 // Lesson 14.1: Enterprise Capstone I: System Architecture & Non-Functional Requirements\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Author the complete System Architecture Specification and ADR documentation; pass an initial archite\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "14.1", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Author the complete System Architecture Specification and ADR documentation; pass an initial architectural review.", "subtopics": ["14.1.1 Defining the enterprise project charter: building a multi-tenant, autonomous software intelligence and operations platform", "14.1.2 Scoping functional requirements: automated code review, security remediation, real-time telemetry analytics, autonomous bug repair", "14.1.3 Scoping non-functional requirements: $99.99\\%$ uptime SLA, sub-200ms API p99 latency, zero cross-tenant data leakage", "14.1.4 Architecture Decision Records (ADRs): documenting technology choices across database, frontend, messaging, and AI models", "14.1.5 Threat modeling: defining security boundaries, zero-trust network policies, and PII compliance controls", "14.1.6 Creating the multi-repository vs monorepo workspace structure with Turborepo and strict dependency boundaries"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Vague architectural boundaries leading to monolithic spaghetti code and circular dependencies between services.", "How does Enterprise Capstone I: System Architecture & Non-Functional Requirements scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-14-2', 'phase-14-lesson-02-enterprise-capstone-ii-relational-data-mo', 'module-14', 22, 'Lesson 14.22: Enterprise Capstone II: Relational Data Modeling & Multi-Tenant Isolation', 'Prerequisites: Phase 5, Phase 10', 'Prerequisites: Phase 5, Phase 10 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 380, 15, 3620.0, 100.0, '# Lesson 14.2: Enterprise Capstone II: Relational Data Modeling & Multi-Tenant Isolation

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 5, Phase 10
- **Subtopics**:
  - `14.2.1` Designing the core relational schema in PostgreSQL: organizations, users, workspaces, repositories, agent tasks, audits
  - `14.2.2` Multi-tenant isolation patterns: Row-Level Security (RLS) vs schema-per-tenant vs database-per-tenant trade-offs
  - `14.2.3` Implementing PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS): policies enforcing `WHERE organization_id = current_setting(''app.current_org_id'')`
  - `14.2.4` Migration pipeline: automated, backward-compatible migrations with Flyway/Prisma/Alembic in continuous delivery pipelines
  - `14.2.5` High-performance indexing: composite B-trees, GIN indexes on JSONB metadata, and partial indexes for active tasks
  - `14.2.6` Seeding the database: generating deterministic, realistic enterprise fixture data with millions of mock records for stress testing
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Leaking tenant data due to missing RLS policies or forgotten `organization_id` filters on complex join queries.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement and verify PostgreSQL Row-Level Security across 15 relational tables with automated multi-tenant isolation unit tests.
- **Project Application**: Enterprise Capstone: Relational database schema with RLS.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 14 // Lesson 14.2: Enterprise Capstone II: Relational Data Modeling & Multi-Tenant Isolation\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement and verify PostgreSQL Row-Level Security across 15 relational tables with automated multi-\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "14.2", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Implement and verify PostgreSQL Row-Level Security across 15 relational tables with automated multi-tenant isolation unit tests.", "subtopics": ["14.2.1 Designing the core relational schema in PostgreSQL: organizations, users, workspaces, repositories, agent tasks, audits", "14.2.2 Multi-tenant isolation patterns: Row-Level Security (RLS) vs schema-per-tenant vs database-per-tenant trade-offs", "14.2.3 Implementing PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS): policies enforcing `WHERE organization_id = current_setting(''app.current_org_id'')`", "14.2.4 Migration pipeline: automated, backward-compatible migrations with Flyway/Prisma/Alembic in continuous delivery pipelines", "14.2.5 High-performance indexing: composite B-trees, GIN indexes on JSONB metadata, and partial indexes for active tasks", "14.2.6 Seeding the database: generating deterministic, realistic enterprise fixture data with millions of mock records for stress testing"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Leaking tenant data due to missing RLS policies or forgotten `organization_id` filters on complex join queries.", "How does Enterprise Capstone II: Relational Data Modeling & Multi-Tenant Isolation scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-14-3', 'phase-14-lesson-03-enterprise-capstone-iii-high-throughput-e', 'module-14', 23, 'Lesson 14.23: Enterprise Capstone III: High-Throughput Event Streaming & Task Queues', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 5, Phase 7', 'Prerequisites: Phase 4, Phase 5, Phase 7 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 380, 15, 3500.0, 150.0, '# Lesson 14.3: Enterprise Capstone III: High-Throughput Event Streaming & Task Queues

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 4, Phase 5, Phase 7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `14.3.1` Asynchronous event backbone: deploying Apache Kafka KRaft clusters for high-throughput event ingestion
  - `14.3.2` Defining event schemas with Protocol Buffers: strict backward and forward schema compatibility guarantees
  - `14.3.3` Kafka producer pipeline: idempotent producers with `acks=all`, batching, and snappy compression
  - `14.3.4` Kafka consumer architecture: consumer groups, partition assignments, and non-blocking retry topics with exponential backoff
  - `14.3.5` Celery and Redis task queue integration: orchestrating long-running asynchronous background jobs with progress reporting
  - `14.3.6` Dead Letter Queues (DLQ) and alert routing: capturing failed messages, poison pills, and automated administrative replay
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Message loss during consumer restarts; poison pills stalling partition consumption indefinitely without DLQs.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Deploy Kafka and Celery streaming infrastructure; achieve 20,000 events/sec ingestion with zero dropped messages.
- **Project Application**: Enterprise Capstone: Distributed event streaming and task queue pipeline.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 14 // Lesson 14.3: Enterprise Capstone III: High-Throughput Event Streaming & Task Queues\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Deploy Kafka and Celery streaming infrastructure; achieve 20,000 events/sec ingestion with zero drop\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "14.3", "subtopics_count": 6, "verification_criteria": "Deploy Kafka and Celery streaming infrastructure; achieve 20,000 events/sec ingestion with zero dropped messages.", "subtopics": ["14.3.1 Asynchronous event backbone: deploying Apache Kafka KRaft clusters for high-throughput event ingestion", "14.3.2 Defining event schemas with Protocol Buffers: strict backward and forward schema compatibility guarantees", "14.3.3 Kafka producer pipeline: idempotent producers with `acks=all`, batching, and snappy compression", "14.3.4 Kafka consumer architecture: consumer groups, partition assignments, and non-blocking retry topics with exponential backoff", "14.3.5 Celery and Redis task queue integration: orchestrating long-running asynchronous background jobs with progress reporting", "14.3.6 Dead Letter Queues (DLQ) and alert routing: capturing failed messages, poison pills, and automated administrative replay"]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Message loss during consumer restarts; poison pills stalling partition consumption indefinitely without DLQs.", "How does Enterprise Capstone III: High-Throughput Event Streaming & Task Queues scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-14-4', 'phase-14-lesson-04-enterprise-capstone-iv-hybrid-rag-pipelin', 'module-14', 24, 'Lesson 14.24: Enterprise Capstone IV: Hybrid RAG Pipeline & Vector Search Engine', 'Prerequisites: Phase 10', 'Prerequisites: Phase 10 | Subtopics: 6 items', 'Systems project application', 380, 15, 3560.0, 200.0, '# Lesson 14.4: Enterprise Capstone IV: Hybrid RAG Pipeline & Vector Search Engine

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 10
- **Subtopics**:
  - `14.4.1` Enterprise knowledge indexing: ingesting multi-format documentation, source code repositories, and user support tickets
  - `14.4.2` Layout-aware document parsing: extracting markdown hierarchies, AST symbol graphs, and tabular data
  - `14.4.3` Dual-stream hybrid retrieval: pgvector HNSW dense semantic search + PostgreSQL BM25 full-text search
  - `14.4.4` Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF): combining search result ranks into a unified candidate pool ($k=60$)
  - `14.4.5` Cross-Encoder re-ranking: scoring top-50 candidates down to top-5 high-relevance chunks using BAAI `bge-reranker-large`
  - `14.4.6` Evaluation gate: verifying with EvalKit that retrieval achieves Context Relevance $>0.88$ and Groundedness $>0.96$
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Vector search returning outdated documentation versions due to missing temporal and version metadata filtering.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement and evaluate the end-to-end hybrid RAG retrieval pipeline on 100,000 enterprise documents with sub-150ms latency.
